title: Marker Kinds
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

The `kind` field of a marker tiddler picks one of five matching modes. The kind determines what source text fires the marker, where the marker can fire (block-position vs inline), and which additional fields are honoured.

!! glyph

Single-fire block marker. The open literal appears at the start of a line; the rest of the line, and possibly the following block, becomes the body.

* Position: block.
* Fires on: open literal followed by whitespace.
* Typical use: paragraph wrappers, line-styled containers.
* Notable fields: `paragraph-marker`, `allow-symbol`, `classes`.
* Worked example: [[vocab/default/DOT]].

!! glyph-level

Multi-fire block marker. Repeating the open literal sets the nesting level (1..maxLevel). The engine emits a `wltc-lN` class corresponding to the level.

* Position: block.
* Fires on: 1..N repetitions of the open literal followed by whitespace.
* Typical use: nested-indent paragraph wrappers.
* Notable fields: `max-level` (default 4), `allow-symbol`.
* Worked example: [[vocab/default/ANGLE]].

!! word

Keyword block marker. The open literal is the keyword itself; whatever follows (single line in inline mode, multiple blocks in block mode) is the body.

* Position: block.
* Fires on: open literal at the start of a line.
* Typical use: section markers, keyword commands (Fountain transitions, markdown headings).
* Notable fields: `mode` (inline vs block), `emit-open`, `end-string`, `paragraph-marker`, `allow-classes`.
* Worked example: [[vocab/fountain/INT]].

!! inline-pair

Bracketed inline marker. Open literal starts the body; matching close literal ends it.

* Position: inline.
* Fires on: open literal at any inline position.
* Typical use: emphasis (italic, bold), code spans, inline brackets.
* Notable fields: `close` (required), `body-raw`, `allow-symbol`.
* Worked example: [[vocab/markdown/CODE]].

!! list-item

List bullet marker. Consecutive matches collapse into a single container (ul/ol/dl/blockquote), nesting via indent.

* Position: block.
* Fires on: optional indent + open literal (or `open-pattern` regex) + whitespace, at line start.
* Typical use: markdown list bullets, definition lists, checkbox lists.
* Notable fields: `container`, `item-element`, `indent-unit`, `open-pattern`.
* Worked example: [[vocab/markdown/ITEM-DASH]].

